          Cinder block will take paint, but not the way drywall does. Its surface is pitted and porous, it is strongly alkaline, and in a basement it is often damp from behind. Roll ordinary wall paint onto it and you get pinholes, a blotchy finish that drinks gallon after gallon, and peeling within a few seasons. The fix is to treat block paint as a system: fill the pores, use a topcoat made for masonry, and make sure the wall is dry enough to hold it.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />


        <!-- Picks -->
