          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The 6 Best Paints and Fillers for Cinder Block, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            These picks cover each layer of a block paint system: fillers, topcoats and waterproofing paints for damp walls. Rankings weigh what the manufacturers publish (solids, alkali tolerance, coverage on block, moisture and pressure ratings, interior or exterior approval), how easy each is to find, and recurring patterns in owner feedback. We did not test these products; specifications are attributed to their makers.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} index={index} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="why-fails" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why Ordinary Wall Paint Fails on Block
          </h2>

          <p>
            The first problem is texture. The face of a concrete block is covered in tiny voids and pits. A standard interior latex is thin enough to bridge over them, leaving air pockets that show as pinholes once the paint dries. Those pinholes look bad, and on an exterior or basement wall they let water straight through to the block.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second problem is chemistry. Concrete, mortar and block are strongly alkaline, especially when new. Paints not formulated for masonry can react with that alkalinity, a process painters call alkali burn or saponification, which leaves soft, discolored or flaking paint. Masonry primers and paints are formulated to tolerate high pH; ordinary wall paint is not.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third problem is moisture. Block absorbs and transmits water readily, and a painted film traps that water at the surface. On a basement wall or a retaining wall with wet soil behind it, moisture pushes on the paint from behind, dissolves salts in the block and leaves them under the film as efflorescence. The paint blisters and lifts off in sheets.
          </p>

          <p>
            Each problem has its own fix: a block filler for texture, an alkali-tolerant masonry topcoat for chemistry, and either drainage or a waterproofing paint for moisture. Skipping any one usually means the paint job fails, regardless of how expensive the topcoat was.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="system" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Three-Layer System: Filler, Topcoat, Moisture Control
          </h2>

          <p>
            Think of a painted block wall as three layers that each solve one problem. The <strong>block filler</strong> is a high-solids primer that fills voids and gives the wall a continuous, sealed surface. The <strong>topcoat</strong> provides color, sheen and weather resistance. And <strong>moisture control</strong> is either drainage that keeps the wall dry or a waterproofing paint that resists water from behind.
          </p>

          <p>
            On a dry interior wall, such as a garage, workshop or above-grade room, you need only the first two: block filler, then two coats of an acrylic masonry or high-quality interior latex topcoat. On an exterior above-grade wall, the same system works with an exterior acrylic masonry topcoat. On a basement wall with any dampness, the waterproofing layer changes the plan.
          </p>

          <p>
            For basement block, a masonry waterproofer like DRYLOK replaces both the filler and the moisture layer, because it is designed to fill pores while resisting water pressure. It can serve as the finish coat itself, often tinted, or be topcoated with latex once cured. Our guide to the <a href="/best-basement-wall-sealer/">best basement wall sealers</a> compares waterproofers if moisture is the bigger concern than color.
          </p>

