        <div id="filler" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Block Filler: The Layer Most People Skip
          </h2>

          <p>
            Block filler, sometimes labeled block surfacer or masonry filler, is a very thick acrylic primer with a high percentage of solids. Applied with a thick-nap roller and worked in, it fills the voids in the block face and leaves a sealed, relatively smooth surface. It looks almost like a thin coat of plaster when it goes on, and it is the reason professionally painted block looks smooth rather than pocked.
          </p>

          <p>
            It also saves money. Bare block can absorb two or three coats of topcoat before the color evens out, and each coat still leaves pinholes. One coat of filler, which usually costs less per gallon than premium topcoat, closes the surface so two coats of topcoat give full, even coverage. On a large wall, the filler often pays for itself in topcoat you do not need.
          </p>

          <p>
            Apply it with a 3/4-inch to 1-1/4-inch nap roller, loaded heavily, and back-brush or work it into the surface so it fills rather than bridges. Some painters spray it and back-roll. Expect coverage far lower than a normal primer, commonly around 50 to 100 square feet per gallon depending on block texture. Split-face and very porous block may need two coats.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ask the paint counter to tint the filler toward your topcoat color. It costs nothing and helps the finish coats cover evenly, especially when going dark over gray block. Allow the full recoat time on the label before topcoating; heavy filler coats take longer to dry than thin primers, particularly in cool basements.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE STEP THAT MATTERS</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Filler first, every time.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">On bare block, one coat of block filler does more for the finished look than any premium topcoat. Skip it and the wall will show pinholes no matter how many coats of paint go on top.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="topcoat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Choosing a Topcoat: Acrylic, Elastomeric or Waterproofer
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>100 percent acrylic masonry paint</strong> is the standard topcoat for block. It is flexible, breathes enough to let some vapor through, resists alkali and UV, and comes in a full range of colors. Products sold as masonry, stucco and brick paint fall into this group. They work inside and out over a block filler.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Elastomeric masonry coatings</strong> are much thicker and more flexible, able to stretch across hairline cracks and bridge small movement. They are excellent on exterior block that cracks along mortar joints or in climates with large temperature swings. They are less breathable, so they are a poor choice on walls with moisture coming from behind, such as basement interiors and retaining walls.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Masonry waterproofers</strong> such as DRYLOK and KILZ basement products are designed for walls that face water from the back. They are cement- or latex-based coatings that fill pores and, in many cases, carry a manufacturer pressure rating. They are the right choice for interior basement block with dampness, but their color and sheen options are limited compared with acrylic topcoats.
          </p>

          <p>
            For sheen, flat or low-sheen finishes hide the texture of block best; satin is easier to clean and suits garages and workshops. Gloss on block emphasizes every imperfection. If you need a floor-to-wall finish in a basement or garage, coordinate with a floor coating; our guide to the <a href="/best-basement-floor-paint/">best basement floor paint</a> covers that side.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="in-out" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Interior vs. Exterior Block: What Changes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Exterior block faces sun, rain, freeze-thaw cycles and wind-driven water. The topcoat must be rated for exterior use, resist UV fading and stay flexible as the wall heats and cools. An exterior acrylic masonry paint over block filler is the standard. Where block cracks, an elastomeric coating is worth the extra cost. Do not use interior paint outside; it chalks and fails quickly in sunlight.
          </p>

          <p>
            Interior above-grade block, such as a garage or a block-wall room, has the easiest job. Moisture is usually low and UV is minimal, so block filler followed by a quality acrylic latex topcoat works well. Garages benefit from a scrubbable satin finish and a light color to reflect light.
          </p>

          <p>
            Interior below-grade block is the hardest. Water comes from behind, the wall is cool so humid air condenses on it, and efflorescence is common. Painting here starts with drainage and moisture testing, then a masonry waterproofer rather than standard paint. For damp walls that you would rather leave unpainted, a penetrating sealer is the alternative; see our ranking of the best <a href="/best-cinder-block-sealer-reviews/">cinder block sealers</a>.
          </p>

