        <div id="new-block" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Painting New Block: Cure Time and Alkalinity
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fresh block and mortar contain a lot of water and are highly alkaline. Paint makers commonly ask for new masonry to cure for about 28 to 30 days before coating, and some products specify longer. Painting too soon traps moisture and exposes the paint to very high pH, which can cause blistering and alkali burn.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some masonry primers are formulated for high-pH surfaces and allow earlier painting; their data sheets list a maximum pH, often in the range of 12 to 13. If you need to paint new block quickly, choose such a primer and test the surface with pH strips on a wetted area. Otherwise, waiting a month is the cheapest insurance you can buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            New block often develops a bloom of efflorescence in its first few months as construction water works its way out. Let it appear, then brush it off and clean with a masonry cleaner before painting. Painting over fresh efflorescence, or before it has finished appearing, sets up the classic failure where salts push paint off the wall.
          </p>

          <p>
            On new construction, plan the drainage before paint: footing drains, gutters, downspout extensions and grading that slopes away from the wall. A painted exterior block wall with roof water dumping at its base will show moisture damage regardless of the paint quality.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="moisture" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Moisture Check: The Test Before the Paint
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before painting any basement or ground-contact block, tape an 18-inch square of clear plastic tightly to the bare wall and leave it 24 to 48 hours. Moisture on the wall side of the plastic means water is coming through the block. Moisture on the room side means condensation from humid air. No moisture means the wall is a reasonable candidate for standard filler and topcoat.
          </p>

          <p>
            If moisture shows on the wall side, do not use ordinary paint. Fix what you can outside first: clean gutters, extend downspouts and correct grading. Then use a masonry waterproofer designed to resist water from behind. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-waterproof-a-basement/">how to waterproof a basement</a> lays out the order of fixes in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            If moisture shows on the room side, run a dehumidifier to bring basement humidity below 60 percent, ideally 30 to 50 percent, before and after painting. Paint applied to a cold, sweating wall will not bond well and will stay soft longer. Painting in a cooler, drier season or after several days of dehumidification gives the best result.
          </p>

          <p>
            Also look for white efflorescence, dark damp bands near the floor, and peeling or blistered old paint. Any of these means moisture has been moving through the wall. Address it first; painting over the evidence just resets the clock on the next failure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="old-paint" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Repainting Block That Is Already Painted
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start by testing adhesion. Press a strip of duct tape firmly onto the old paint, then rip it off quickly. If paint comes off with the tape, the old coating is failing and must be removed before repainting. If the tape comes away clean, the existing paint is sound and can be cleaned, scuffed and repainted with a compatible primer and topcoat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Remove failing paint with a wire brush, scraper, paint stripper or, on exterior walls, a pressure washer used carefully so it does not erode mortar. A grinder with a wire cup wheel is fast but creates a lot of dust; wear a respirator. If the old paint might be from before 1978, test it for lead before sanding or grinding and follow EPA lead-safe practices if it tests positive.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once the wall is down to sound paint or bare block, wash it with a masonry cleaner or a mild detergent, rinse and let it dry. Spot-prime bare areas with block filler, and use a bonding primer over any glossy old paint. Then apply two topcoats. Masonry waterproofers generally need bare masonry, so on a basement wall you may have to strip it entirely to switch to a waterproofer.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the old paint keeps peeling in the same spots, look for the cause before repainting: a leaking gutter, a downspout at the base of the wall, a crack letting water in, or humidity condensing in a cold corner. Paint fails where water is, and fixing that is what makes the new coat last.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Tools, Coats and Coverage
          </h2>

          <p>
            Block needs heavier tools than drywall. Use a thick-nap roller, 3/4-inch for smooth block and up to 1-1/4-inch for rough or split-face block, plus a stiff nylon brush for mortar joints, corners and edges. An airless sprayer speeds up large walls, but back-roll the first coat so filler and paint are worked into the surface rather than sitting on it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Coverage on block is much lower than paint can labels suggest, because the surface area of a pitted block face is far greater than its flat dimensions. Block filler often covers 50 to 100 square feet per gallon. Topcoats over a filled surface usually reach 200 to 300 square feet per gallon. Masonry waterproofers land around 75 to 100 square feet per gallon on the first coat.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Block Painting by the Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">28-30 days</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical cure before painting new block</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">50-100</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sq ft per gallon, block filler</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">200-300</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sq ft per gallon, topcoat over filler</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1 + 2</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Filler coat plus topcoats</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Work in moderate conditions. Most acrylic masonry products specify surface and air temperatures above about 50 degrees Fahrenheit, and some allow down to 35 degrees; check the label. Avoid painting exterior walls in direct midday sun, before rain or late in the day when dew may settle on fresh paint. Indoors, ventilate and keep humidity down so coats dry properly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Count on one coat of filler and two of topcoat as the normal specification. Heavily textured block may need a second filler coat. Inspect each coat in raking light from a work lamp held flat to the wall; pinholes that show light need dabbing before the next coat.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. What Painting a Block Wall Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            At the time of writing, block filler typically runs roughly $25 to $50 per gallon, acrylic masonry topcoats roughly $35 to $70 per gallon, elastomeric coatings roughly $45 to $90 per gallon, and masonry waterproofers roughly $35 to $60 per gallon. Premium contractor-grade lines cost more but often have better published specifications for alkali and moisture tolerance.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a 500-square-foot block wall, a full filler-plus-two-topcoats system usually needs around 5 to 10 gallons of filler and 4 to 5 gallons of topcoat, roughly $300 to $650 in materials. A two-coat waterproofer system on a damp basement wall of the same size needs about 10 to 12 gallons, roughly $400 to $700.
          </p>

