        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the block filler</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Topcoat alone bridges over the voids in block and leaves pinholes, a blotchy finish and a wall that soaks up extra coats. One coat of a high-solids block filler, worked in with a thick-nap roller, closes the surface so two topcoats give even, durable color. It usually saves topcoat money too.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting new block too soon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                New block and mortar hold construction water and are highly alkaline for weeks. Paint applied too early traps that moisture and suffers alkali burn, which shows as soft, discolored or peeling paint. Wait about 28 to 30 days, or use a primer rated for high-pH surfaces.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting a damp basement wall</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ordinary paint on a basement wall with moisture coming through blisters and peels as water and salts push behind it. Run a plastic sheet test first. If the wall side gets wet, fix outdoor drainage and use a masonry waterproofer instead of standard paint and filler.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using interior paint outside</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Interior wall paint is not built for UV, rain and freeze-thaw cycles and chalks and fails quickly on exterior block. Use a 100 percent acrylic exterior masonry paint or an elastomeric coating rated for exterior use, over a compatible block filler or masonry primer suited to the surface.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting over efflorescence</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The white salts on block keep forming as moisture moves through the wall and will push any paint off from behind. Brush them away dry, clean with an efflorescence remover, rinse and let the wall dry. If the salts reappear quickly, fix the moisture before painting.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which type of paint holds up best on cinder block?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For dry block, inside or out, the most durable general-purpose system is a high-solids block filler topped with two coats of 100 percent acrylic masonry paint. That combination is the safe default. For exterior walls that crack, an elastomeric masonry coating adds flexibility. For basement walls with dampness, a masonry waterproofer such as DRYLOK is the better choice than standard paint.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I skip block filler and just use more topcoat?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can, but it rarely saves money or looks as good. Topcoat does not have enough solids to fill the voids in block, so even three or four coats leave pinholes and an uneven sheen. One coat of block filler closes the surface and usually reduces topcoat to two coats, which is cheaper and gives a better finish.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can elastomeric paint go on basement block walls?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is generally not recommended on the inside of below-grade walls. Elastomeric coatings are less breathable, and moisture from the soil side can push them off as blisters. They are best on exterior above-grade block that cracks. For basement interiors, use a masonry waterproofer if the wall is damp or an acrylic system over filler if it is dry.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long must fresh block sit before it can be painted?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most paint makers recommend about 28 to 30 days for new block and mortar, and some ask for longer. During that time construction water evaporates and the surface alkalinity begins to drop. If you must paint sooner, use a masonry primer rated for high-pH surfaces and check the surface pH with test strips first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much paint does a cinder block wall take?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Far more than drywall. Block filler often covers only 50 to 100 square feet per gallon, and topcoat over filler about 200 to 300 square feet per gallon. For a 500-square-foot wall, plan on roughly 5 to 10 gallons of filler and 4 to 5 gallons of topcoat. Rough or split-face block uses more.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What sheen is best for painted block?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Flat or low-sheen finishes hide the texture and imperfections of block best and suit most exterior walls and living spaces. Satin is easier to wipe clean and a good choice for garages, workshops and basements used for storage. High gloss on block highlights every pit and joint and is rarely a good look.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I paint over block that was sealed with a repellent?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often not. Many penetrating silane-siloxane repellents leave the surface too water-repellent for paint to bond. Splash water on the wall; if it beads strongly, paint adhesion is likely to be poor. Check the sealer data sheet for paint compatibility, wait for the treatment to wear off, or test adhesion on a small patch before painting the whole wall.
              </p>
